#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151837 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "58f2bf06-7449-5a66-9662-532ce70a8ff9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21879" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation below, for which the corresponding complementary solution is $y_c(x)=Ax^6+Bx^2,$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants.\n\n$$\nx^2y'' - 7xy' + 12y = -3x^3\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $y_p(x)=x$ or $y_p(x)=x^3$ is a particular solution of the differential equation, and then write the general solution." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y_c(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- $y_p(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For $y_p(x)=x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}=1, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(1)^{′}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\nx^2(0) - 7x(1) + 12(x) = -3x^3. \\:\\: {\\color{red}\\times}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_p(x)=x$ isn't a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For $y_p(x)=x^3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}=3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2})^{′}=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\nx^2(6x) - 7x(3x^2) + 12(x^3) = -3x^3. \\:\\: {\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_p(x)=x^3$ is a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is $y_c(x)=Ax^6+Bx^2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{x}^{6}+B{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151909 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67ca0bc-c946-5963-a733-2ec9eb6b07ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/id #uuid "bacb284c-88db-5ad5-bd7d-cbe6fbc604df" :question/math-academy-id "e-6789" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{5x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 10y' + 25y = 0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let $y_{2}=u(x) e^{5x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}={e}^{5x}({u}^{′}+5u) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u)-10{e}^{5x}({u}^{′}+5u)+25{e}^{5x}u & =0 \\\\ {e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u-10{u}^{′}-50u+25u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{5x}({u}^{″}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{5x}\\neq 0$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu(x) = c_1x+c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(x) = ue^{5x}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\left(c_1 x + c_2\\right).\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\cdot x = xe^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W(y_1, y_2) = e^{10x} \\neq 0,$ we know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the equation is\n\n$$\ny=Ae^{5x}+Bxe^{5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151911 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "bbf72227-f0da-5dab-88cd-bfed2ed773f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-6792" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $y'' - 3y'+ 2y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y_1(x) = e^{x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- $y_2(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement.\n\n- To determine whether $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y_1$ and its derivatives into the equation. Computing the first and second derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n\n$$\ne^{x}  - 3e^{x} + 2e^{x} = 0. \\color{green}\\quad\\checkmark\n$$\n\nSo $y_1$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of $y_2$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n\n$$\n4e^{2x}  - 3(2e^{2x})  + 2(e^{2x} ) = 0. \\quad\\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\nSo $y_2$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- Both $y_1=e^x$ and $y_2=e^{2x}$ are solutions of the equation. So, the general solution is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}$ if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. To check if they are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}{({e}^{2x})}^{′}-{({e}^{x})}^{′}{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian does not equal $0,$ the functions are linearly independent. Therefore, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct statements are I, II, and III."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151912 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "f41bad20-5a72-5d6d-82c4-cf36f7ac74cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6794" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation $x^2 y'' + 3xy' + y = 0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =u(x){y}_{1}(x) \\\\  & =u(x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{u(x)}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y_{2} = \\dfrac{u(x)}{x}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =\\frac{u}{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}x-u}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & =\\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(\\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u}{{x}^{3}})+3x(\\frac{{u}^{′}x-u}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{u}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u+3{u}^{′}x-3u+u}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}+{u}^{′}x}{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}x+{u}^{′} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation:\n\n$$\nw' x + w = 0\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = \\dfrac{c_1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = \\dfrac{c_1}{x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = c_1\\ln{x} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(x) = \\dfrac{u}{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{c_1\\ln{x} + c_2}{x}.\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{\\ln{x}}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W(y_1, y_2) = \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\neq 0,$ we know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{A}{x} + \\dfrac{B\\ln{x}}{x} = \\dfrac{A + B\\ln{x}}{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67ca0bc-c946-5963-a733-2ec9eb6b07ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d005f2e4-bdb4-539e-9319-3dd773e75374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345424} {:answer/id #uuid "b5cf3c07-f28f-5d10-81d1-7e1b191c5adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345425} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd43338-b42d-5aef-9330-1b0efc2d9f45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345426} {:answer/id #uuid "b37b7654-37e8-52f2-85f9-8720ecb27b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345427} {:answer/id #uuid "e829a974-05bc-5521-b96a-620251681971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dd43338-b42d-5aef-9330-1b0efc2d9f45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345426} :answer-field/id #uuid "97c17e77-38a2-5835-b825-8c5cd3818c0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2001423a-d37a-5c09-b979-2bab1292dbaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-129942" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})-2({u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}-2u{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{2x}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67ca0bc-c946-5963-a733-2ec9eb6b07ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e58b3dd-a3b7-5da9-b4d6-a02626dc371a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2t}+Bt{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345431} {:answer/id #uuid "d3836d7a-f6ee-5631-8faa-2573ee12135f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345432} {:answer/id #uuid "5880b1f2-cf03-5a6e-bc6c-2f580523a189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345433} {:answer/id #uuid "397ece8b-d486-5771-a65c-269cde3e638d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345434} {:answer/id #uuid "78ea7268-48ad-5be8-a7f8-8832dcae05fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78ea7268-48ad-5be8-a7f8-8832dcae05fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345435} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b9fdc43-d5e0-52de-b2b7-a3656f2ed21f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5120476-e026-5368-b3db-664e0d6dd1eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-129956" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-2t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){e}^{-2t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}-2u{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}+4u{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}+4u{e}^{-2t})+5({u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}-2u{e}^{-2t})+6u{e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}+4u{e}^{-2t}+5{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}-10u{e}^{-2t}+6u{e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}+{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-2t}({u}^{″}+{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}+w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\,⟹\\,u=-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{e}^{-2t}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}){e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{-2t}={e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-{e}^{-5t}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21ca1c98-39d6-58fc-a052-7fd4f4baf3ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345438} {:answer/id #uuid "044f3745-e5e5-5f33-9c7f-8a79f58b5df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345439} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c67792-2047-5297-a989-42e219275894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345440} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb3d7e8-7156-5b95-a41e-ed711ca7d69a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345441} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ee4f66-6b9c-5f9a-b343-aa1a082677b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21ca1c98-39d6-58fc-a052-7fd4f4baf3ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345438} :answer-field/id #uuid "12818fef-4f6e-5d11-af5d-66cde3336978" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1d410c0-047c-5f49-a378-14e326ad53fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-130003" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=Ax+Bx\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =1 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(0)-x(1)+x=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x})-x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(x)(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}-(x)^{′}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-(1)x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not equal to $0,$ the functions are linearly independent, hence $y=Ax+Bx\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$  is the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87502948-1837-5a40-a865-25c54b543f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345445} {:answer/id #uuid "97bf1bb4-c458-5c49-ba18-5f6c6d011526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345446} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c4d0ae-8c3e-5e0f-940a-c70f97c87ed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345447} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c0cd14-7d4a-5f7c-bd4a-f8a42411ebd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345448} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2a1047-eaa3-52d0-b208-e2fe3487929b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c2a1047-eaa3-52d0-b208-e2fe3487929b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345449} :answer-field/id #uuid "fae05613-a3fa-5761-81f5-ba92b39aebb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5007768b-5de4-5206-bbd7-b32aa44bd388" :question/math-academy-id "q-130005" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $x{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+4{x}^{3}y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is a solution to the equation\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\nx(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=(4{x}^{3}-4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is false.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\nx(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2})-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}=(2x-2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}+(4{x}^{3}-4{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution while ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution of the equation, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22e30927-b475-55e2-8df9-d99b889b8e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{A}{t}+Bt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345452} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b2eaf2-55a0-5e5d-b855-0ab1b9b71566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345453} {:answer/id #uuid "bbfbb4c9-66b3-59c6-a96f-d00ed4335d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345454} {:answer/id #uuid "c3bdca4b-33bb-55e4-b8b0-0d31cc358add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\sqrt[3]{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345455} {:answer/id #uuid "5def96eb-61e7-5671-990a-4e0cfb7a11f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\sqrt{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5def96eb-61e7-5671-990a-4e0cfb7a11f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\sqrt{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345456} :answer-field/id #uuid "81ad463b-45d7-5172-b18f-aa25b2851e3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78c4435d-d549-59c7-b588-14428a93b097" :question/math-academy-id "q-130012" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}$ is a solution to the equation $2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+3t{y}^{′}-y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{-1}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{-1}-u{t}^{-2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{-1}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{-2}+2u{t}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{-1}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{-2}+2u{t}^{-3})+3t({u}^{′}{t}^{-1}-u{t}^{-2})-u{t}^{-1} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}t-4{u}^{′}+4u{t}^{-1}+3{u}^{′}-3u{t}^{-1}-u{t}^{-1} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}t-{u}^{′} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n2t{w}^{′}-w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\sqrt{t}\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{t}^{3/2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{-1}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{t}^{3/2}+{c}_{2}){t}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3/2}\\cdot {t}^{-1}={t}^{1/2}=\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\sqrt{t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef1e0dc-62ff-5bd0-990a-8fb1b5cc6f4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+\\frac{B}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345459} {:answer/id #uuid "804f03ca-e55e-5f8d-9f7c-23952eb833e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+Bt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345460} {:answer/id #uuid "a668455f-10cc-5fad-ba36-537a4f50d8bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+\\frac{B}{\\sqrt{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345461} {:answer/id #uuid "a4186bac-e04c-5d30-b055-ba5b65a8aa79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+B\\sqrt[3]{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345462} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3efed7-029a-5b24-b608-9a9dc4398a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f3efed7-029a-5b24-b608-9a9dc4398a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345463} :answer-field/id #uuid "5df7d2ed-a137-509a-be4b-c254ca46333c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b07ce313-36a3-5fa3-94a0-d88524a33c9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-130017" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-4t{y}^{′}+6y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u)-4t({u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut)+6u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4}+4{u}^{′}{t}^{3}+2u{t}^{2}-4{u}^{′}{t}^{3}-8u{t}^{2}+6u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=({c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}){t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=t\\cdot {t}^{2}={t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={t}^{4}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87644de6-219d-5466-bf46-84f24ad6aed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345466} {:answer/id #uuid "4c1a4662-08a9-5568-aa2e-6073e37cbd1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{5/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345467} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1fc8ce-0359-5500-a620-0ced8dea5a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345468} {:answer/id #uuid "e0cc3241-1cbb-54dd-a41a-7daed0977005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{7/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345469} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb1932c-268e-5d36-af1b-0a92cc6f22e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{4/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c1a4662-08a9-5568-aa2e-6073e37cbd1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{5/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345467} :answer-field/id #uuid "aed909bc-e003-59a0-a394-761de917a81c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "731e581f-8619-54fb-8401-87d512e8b348" :question/math-academy-id "q-130040" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-15y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)+x({u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2})-15u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{5}+12{u}^{′}{x}^{4}+12u{x}^{3}+{u}^{′}{x}^{4}+3u{x}^{3}-15u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{5}+13{u}^{′}{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}(2{u}^{″}x+13{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n2{u}^{″}x+13{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n2{w}^{′}x+13w=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-13/2}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-13/2}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{11}{x}^{-11/2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=(-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{11}{x}^{-11/2}+{c}_{2}){x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-\\frac{11}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-11/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-\\frac{11}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{5/2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd2dc29f-4f06-5244-8541-524de836ba32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345473} {:answer/id #uuid "322261ca-bca5-5ef5-83b7-a16255d6dfd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd2dc29f-4f06-5244-8541-524de836ba32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345473} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ef30e6-dac9-5cb3-a138-7d3370bd4dd2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345472} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "affc6062-4dae-531b-875c-96e77180f35a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345476} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab340fa-fa83-5f3d-a410-5fd0cb106163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ab340fa-fa83-5f3d-a410-5fd0cb106163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345477} :answer-field/id #uuid "450af932-b41b-5a38-9d11-4b3dc34b6b9c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345475} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f853851-f6ce-5eb0-947e-118d6bb4b346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345479} {:answer/id #uuid "28c88f90-dbc5-504e-abfb-4148d1bbfaee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345480} {:answer/id #uuid "c045dcb9-6558-5954-a6cd-328cae6f62b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345481} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3fa8df-1e78-5502-ae15-71fc5a2e79a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd3fa8df-1e78-5502-ae15-71fc5a2e79a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345482} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcd935af-60f1-5ab5-8ba6-f5d6ab5235d7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345478} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8ab9268-0ff7-512c-a816-77237bd4a85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345484} {:answer/id #uuid "f95f1049-b584-5290-a6ad-e79d154c90c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345485} {:answer/id #uuid "8b991e79-eeee-5a2d-989b-652e6dd0d78a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345486} {:answer/id #uuid "ba248c3c-1893-503f-8287-8667fa417ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cx{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f95f1049-b584-5290-a6ad-e79d154c90c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345485} :answer-field/id #uuid "68a9a607-9c9f-54ac-89d8-23cc61f2a56a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345483} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ff2963f-9d17-5d8b-a5d2-82818bbeee3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345489} {:answer/id #uuid "5d31126b-8285-5c01-af4e-59fae9e3875d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ff2963f-9d17-5d8b-a5d2-82818bbeee3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345489} :answer-field/id #uuid "339374eb-b1de-5449-90b3-8b40df7fdcf3" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f225fe36-b845-56ed-a087-0e870bbc744e" :question/math-academy-id "q-339876" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y=3{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{-x})^{′}=-{e}^{-x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(-{e}^{-x})^{′}={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n({e}^{-x})+5(-{e}^{-x})+4({e}^{-x})=3{e}^{-x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{-x})^{′}={e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x})^{′}=-2{e}^{-x}+x{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(-2{e}^{-x}+x{e}^{-x})+5({e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x})+4(x{e}^{-x})=3{e}^{-x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x}+x{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2f9cb26-0d59-5a39-b6b1-066e889ae583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345493} {:answer/id #uuid "29495cc3-1d3a-53a0-8f08-721d7f1e4eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29495cc3-1d3a-53a0-8f08-721d7f1e4eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345494} :answer-field/id #uuid "032465bc-da09-5950-b45e-10eb8788105a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345492} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af497aff-3607-52ab-8343-8192ab90cb1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345496} {:answer/id #uuid "fa32d4d1-4781-54e5-aee4-6b5e01600f73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af497aff-3607-52ab-8343-8192ab90cb1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345496} :answer-field/id #uuid "49f60ed0-4ec2-5441-aaaf-18a5460c5e3e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345495} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1f53183-0942-51dd-a75e-5829e8e8f580" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345499} {:answer/id #uuid "5978f79c-6458-5375-bdca-e5292dc19bce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345500} {:answer/id #uuid "4161de30-cfa9-5783-8580-0e9213a88895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345501} {:answer/id #uuid "690e09be-68ed-5c2e-82d0-c678a3ec3629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{2x}+B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5978f79c-6458-5375-bdca-e5292dc19bce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345500} :answer-field/id #uuid "86e991d6-e332-5bb8-91b8-75d036f1368f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345498} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7829808d-5268-580e-94a9-25d618fcca37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345504} {:answer/id #uuid "34518e9a-664d-510d-aaba-3a5b973ac0e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345505} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4d74e9-fb14-583e-bcbf-90c2f509708a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345506} {:answer/id #uuid "a924a667-d6d4-5f7a-aff3-93325b3c5e08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cx{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd4d74e9-fb14-583e-bcbf-90c2f509708a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345506} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9c5be4f-468c-57cc-8c17-be687ff30247" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345503} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57aeab96-9c25-593c-9c07-686c7fa29ffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345509} {:answer/id #uuid "b39d144d-9813-5bbe-a8d3-7d8086e63721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57aeab96-9c25-593c-9c07-686c7fa29ffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345509} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1e58253-8279-59d5-bba8-20621f570821" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac702672-4400-5de9-a4df-b92fd2822693" :question/math-academy-id "q-339878" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-6y=6{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{3x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{3x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{3x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{3x})^{′}=3{e}^{3x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(3{e}^{3x})^{′}=9{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(9{e}^{3x})+(3{e}^{3x})-6({e}^{3x})=6{e}^{3x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{3x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{3x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{3x})^{′}={e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x})^{′}=6{e}^{3x}+9x{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(6{e}^{3x}+9x{e}^{3x})+({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x})-6(x{e}^{3x})=6{e}^{3x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{3x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93a06f05-2dbe-5d11-b97a-e324e3bf2a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345513} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a25e4a-3a9c-5f17-a87a-62fcda60c6a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2a25e4a-3a9c-5f17-a87a-62fcda60c6a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345514} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee002c3c-0f23-5ff9-84e1-49498632bc15" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345512} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b481ff2-12af-5c8f-9e8a-83cd419468b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345516} {:answer/id #uuid "48f9cced-6c1f-568c-a53b-48e0266993c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b481ff2-12af-5c8f-9e8a-83cd419468b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345516} :answer-field/id #uuid "08784a54-630b-5523-ad61-81936c61aafa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345515} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da3e53d-9b2d-5da9-b5c1-a8122ae5e561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{x}^{4}+B{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345519} {:answer/id #uuid "826c06bc-42ab-5648-93bf-41cd72d7807f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{x}^{3}+B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345520} {:answer/id #uuid "19a8df14-d3cf-5acf-95c1-58898c64b7d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{x}^{3}+Bx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345521} {:answer/id #uuid "3f76b020-df2f-5ea7-9b44-c103fb9240b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{x}^{2}+Bx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "826c06bc-42ab-5648-93bf-41cd72d7807f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{x}^{3}+B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345520} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dd27faf-05de-5aa5-ad57-f429266976a3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345518} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f476a207-0671-56a0-b7e9-5911dc0cea8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345524} {:answer/id #uuid "660136ef-65f8-5d68-867b-055db0ed511e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345525} {:answer/id #uuid "1a37edcc-f373-5d02-8c6b-e78840a12a60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345526} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a304ed-aa71-5b5f-aaf8-3b0901bfd1d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a37edcc-f373-5d02-8c6b-e78840a12a60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345526} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8cbc1e0-70d5-5f76-8191-1d459d911fbc" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345523} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7615af9-9277-5288-8914-4d3153af3fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345529} {:answer/id #uuid "089963a0-6f3a-5093-a310-b6159a8d8f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7615af9-9277-5288-8914-4d3153af3fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345529} :answer-field/id #uuid "689ce391-d260-5a59-a6d0-2e41f80c4219" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af1eb49f-2047-54c5-af74-dcf0eb0576e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-339910" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-2x{y}^{′}=-2x\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{3}+B,$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}=1, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(1)^{′}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(0)-2x(1)=-2x.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}=2x, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(2x)^{′}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(2)-2x(2x)=-2x.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{3}+B.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{x}^{3}+B+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9afe2a22-2ebd-5e35-a966-8789acae5f54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345533} {:answer/id #uuid "ae11eb77-a90a-5f90-bc6c-867c809aa838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae11eb77-a90a-5f90-bc6c-867c809aa838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345534} :answer-field/id #uuid "b543d84b-439c-589e-806a-97621bf1d072" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345532} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c66058c-f28e-5d46-9d73-ce6d3f16706d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345536} {:answer/id #uuid "59a2cd35-9d36-55dc-ae00-9e5096bd1844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c66058c-f28e-5d46-9d73-ce6d3f16706d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345536} :answer-field/id #uuid "029261a8-19fa-55a1-aad9-63635db4f862" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345535} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e51cff1c-31cb-5e5b-863f-9d7ffc280336" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{x}^{4}+B{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345539} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf4cd37-4269-5938-b208-f96d8cb50107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{x}^{3}+Bx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345540} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a8abff-6ac2-5645-bd13-dc0d76422928" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345541} {:answer/id #uuid "26e777d7-4a02-5df3-be68-efdc5246a150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26e777d7-4a02-5df3-be68-efdc5246a150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345542} :answer-field/id #uuid "f64ea463-9129-5fb3-b284-4dbc7d1a872f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345538} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f13723-5618-549c-af2a-12771f1c8b98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345544} {:answer/id #uuid "edd1206e-0108-5b28-8e53-9684e2fea6e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345545} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c4a3ed-0aa2-5897-8343-ef9efedd9bf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345546} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe4b327-2460-51c5-b013-4de27349c1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0c4a3ed-0aa2-5897-8343-ef9efedd9bf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345546} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7be5430-2211-58a2-bf6f-f1c011cfc9fc" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345543} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9a9fae8-6656-5aa2-b0d5-f11f11a4589d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345549} {:answer/id #uuid "5edb219e-c41b-5cca-b246-081a39767960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9a9fae8-6656-5aa2-b0d5-f11f11a4589d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345549} :answer-field/id #uuid "78a12904-bd0f-5b19-8fff-1b00febebd69" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f873b9e-7818-5ec8-8091-26b92885da7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339913" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6x{y}^{′}+10y=4x\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}=1, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(1)^{′}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(0)-6x(1)+10(x)=4x.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}=3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2})^{′}=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(6x)-6x(3{x}^{2})+10({x}^{3})=4x.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2}+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d859af0-eb0d-5a2d-bfa9-bec69abb7ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ax^{4} + Bx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149871} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef1c071-a330-52d7-9dc4-dab21e8ec97a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ax^{5} + Bx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149872} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b43a0d-e608-5ef5-80d2-e00f0f7de94d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ax^{3} + B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149873} {:answer/id #uuid "adb6f873-95f4-5b14-845b-be385bf113e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ax^{5} + B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adb6f873-95f4-5b14-845b-be385bf113e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ax^{5} + B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149874} :answer-field/id #uuid "f31cd40d-ee38-575f-a6e5-a5b2cc1812c8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149870} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "918d8390-f73e-574e-84f6-655ec0ac6201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149876} {:answer/id #uuid "08daca02-7196-522f-b6b0-4e10d92e21e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08daca02-7196-522f-b6b0-4e10d92e21e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149877} :answer-field/id #uuid "e65c611a-1f49-597c-a8d4-190a2eb8f4a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149875} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3a80436-69c4-59ac-aa87-42fcb9698b7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149879} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b51632-afb0-5deb-a07b-b0b3999b408c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cx^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149880} {:answer/id #uuid "17a3dfb3-3fc7-5f4a-8aea-0a0d7cc1530d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149881} {:answer/id #uuid "451eb26c-f634-59a2-ac9b-c25cc78a9bf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17a3dfb3-3fc7-5f4a-8aea-0a0d7cc1530d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149881} :answer-field/id #uuid "6385b0e9-c6d8-5302-b92e-673f0868a626" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149878} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eefd07d-6e2c-56e8-a582-8bf421020325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149884} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5766d4-f645-5a16-a3a9-ea32a7cf8be7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a5766d4-f645-5a16-a3a9-ea32a7cf8be7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149885} :answer-field/id #uuid "71e970f5-1fce-5fcb-a0a3-d5430ddba5ac" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149883} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daae341e-0769-57b7-9bbb-e849e5698fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345552} {:answer/id #uuid "1da3ac10-7b7b-529f-9402-8c2430665216" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daae341e-0769-57b7-9bbb-e849e5698fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345552} :answer-field/id #uuid "28f4a0aa-b592-5c5d-b9f9-77404373105b" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ba452e1-067b-53c9-a55d-7aa33b4eb4d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-339917" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′}=-6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{5}+B,$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}=1, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(1)^{′}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(0)-4x(1)=-6{x}^{2}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}=2x, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(2x)^{′}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(2)-4x(2x)=-6{x}^{2}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{5}+B.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{x}^{5}+B+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67ca0bc-c946-5963-a733-2ec9eb6b07ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fd2d81f-6363-5eb0-a4ad-867b31c6cea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3t}+Bt{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345556} {:answer/id #uuid "0053a237-52c3-590f-aff4-81949b50cf25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3t}+B{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345557} {:answer/id #uuid "2064f67d-9bd4-5e9e-804c-268badee2815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3t}+B{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345558} {:answer/id #uuid "936d1fa3-97a5-5eff-bf30-0c818738a33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3t}+Bt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345559} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1454c0-268d-5bec-9668-4d7bc0b30bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3t}+B{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fd2d81f-6363-5eb0-a4ad-867b31c6cea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3t}+Bt{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345556} :answer-field/id #uuid "7917a17f-497a-5000-a3d2-9b83597ec9ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7059e64a-e7c7-5672-bd6a-16f34aeaebbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-65120" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{3t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){e}^{3t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{3t}+3u{e}^{3t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{3t}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3t}+9u{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{3t}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3t}+9u{e}^{3t})-6({u}^{′}{e}^{3t}+3u{e}^{3t})+9(u{e}^{3t}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{3t}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3t}+9u{e}^{3t}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{3t}-18u{e}^{3t}+9u{e}^{3t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{3t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have:\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}\n$$\n\nThus, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{e}^{3t}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={e}^{3t}({c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={e}^{3t}\\cdot t=t{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{6t}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3t}+Bt{e}^{3t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22fd5242-997f-5163-a30e-42db18763971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345563} {:answer/id #uuid "784414e6-6ca6-5b80-86cf-0d9dbffc192f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345564} {:answer/id #uuid "c163f5a8-7e6c-540a-bca8-09d0bc41a62a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{3}+Bt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345565} {:answer/id #uuid "b39c4523-4585-5400-99cc-d04fb7083755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{3}+B{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345566} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d55a68-cdf4-5607-9d99-2e68cfd74d07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{3}+B{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b39c4523-4585-5400-99cc-d04fb7083755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{3}+B{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345566} :answer-field/id #uuid "50a645b1-2d93-5f7f-89d9-479c20e47dea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d98755dd-1fff-5a9c-839c-b0e549ae372d" :question/math-academy-id "q-65123" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-6t{y}^{′}+12y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{3}.$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+6ut.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+6ut)-6t({u}^{′}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2})+12(u{t}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+6{u}^{′}t+6u-6{u}^{′}t-18u+12u) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{5}({u}^{″}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{5}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}({c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=t\\cdot {t}^{3}={t}^{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={t}^{6}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{t}^{3}+B{t}^{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf0114e-c3da-5f77-bf4e-2049f662a018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345570} {:answer/id #uuid "e605c6ca-e514-5b09-bd16-8ef34cda4fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345571} {:answer/id #uuid "541ff7a0-f988-5200-bbfa-95df094039e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345572} {:answer/id #uuid "20749f07-b461-504a-b2b1-4a44080313f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345573} {:answer/id #uuid "60c8e750-71f9-5373-910d-ef84fff5454d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "541ff7a0-f988-5200-bbfa-95df094039e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345572} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ecebc68-c174-5102-b97e-f4cacc16f40b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58ccb5cc-18e4-5262-8dcb-4e18578a688d" :question/math-academy-id "q-77674" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+4y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={sin}^{2}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B{sin}^{2}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-2{sin}^{2}⁡x+2{cos}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-2{sin}^{2}⁡x+2{cos}^{2}⁡x+4({sin}^{2}⁡x)=2{sin}^{2}⁡x+2{cos}^{2}⁡x=2.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is false.\n\n- So, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution while ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution of the equation, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B{sin}^{2}⁡x$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79be48c9-db1b-54e4-9c06-dd1d4380cc56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345577} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f83a0b-62ed-56ef-9a77-c112be4e20e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345578} {:answer/id #uuid "e014b137-f87e-5727-a4f4-c575320f992e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345579} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e8f23a-6d02-5999-b05a-cfee77655471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345580} {:answer/id #uuid "e8485ca6-6fd5-5979-a335-e327c2daf576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e014b137-f87e-5727-a4f4-c575320f992e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345579} :answer-field/id #uuid "edbdca44-d986-5693-a9da-2d1de48e3591" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4147874-53d3-5715-b4ec-da87549bacb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-79930" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-t}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{1-t}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{1-t}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(t)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t}+2(-{e}^{-t})+{e}^{-t} & =(1-2+1){e}^{-t}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{1-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{1-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-t}+2(-{e}^{1-t})+{e}^{1-t} & =(1-2+1){e}^{1-t}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & ={e}^{-t}{({e}^{1-t})}^{′}-{({e}^{-t})}^{′}{e}^{1-t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(-{e}^{1-t})-(-{e}^{-t}){e}^{1-t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-2t}+{e}^{1-2t} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0,$ the functions are not linearly independent, hence $y=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{1-t}$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67ca0bc-c946-5963-a733-2ec9eb6b07ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6e48b0e-ea41-5e8f-8528-8e942eaff8f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345584} {:answer/id #uuid "4093dd62-d060-513b-93cb-2cd3ba433998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345585} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa7010b-730e-5b92-ac41-e26d6c027f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345586} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c04272-4291-555e-83ee-643104567280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345587} {:answer/id #uuid "a849d5bc-8950-582d-85e3-091352e7f9b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6e48b0e-ea41-5e8f-8528-8e942eaff8f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345584} :answer-field/id #uuid "837fcb19-470e-5255-b32e-1b6facaab254" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62ecae12-793e-56dd-b009-fb63a7fb9bdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-79931" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-4x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{-4x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-4x}-8{u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{-4x}-8{u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4})+8({u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x})+16(u{e}^{-4x}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-4x}-8{u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4x}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-32u{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-4x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-4x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{-4x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}\\cdot x=x{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{-8x}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff60d890-b197-5c18-98df-79c90cd6dde9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345591} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2a365f-431b-5eb9-aa9b-b6057dcdccb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+Bt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345592} {:answer/id #uuid "35ac82b4-9571-5402-8c26-a616784a3076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+B\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345593} {:answer/id #uuid "9638192d-3954-511b-913a-92b9b3a4aa9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345594} {:answer/id #uuid "01dc282e-a75a-5544-a593-b736f265b9c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b2a365f-431b-5eb9-aa9b-b6057dcdccb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{t}^{2}+Bt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345592} :answer-field/id #uuid "2deb90bf-381b-5108-8e73-692ffc2836ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "25b6d0d4-7825-556a-b87b-d3677af04db8" :question/math-academy-id "q-79934" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-2t{y}^{′}+2y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u)-2t({u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut)+2(u{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4}+4{u}^{′}{t}^{3}+2u{t}^{2}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{3}-4u{t}^{2}+2u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}(t{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nt{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\nt{w}^{′}+2w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}+{c}_{2}){t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-{t}^{2}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{t}^{2}+Bt.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345596 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54b0d0a3-21a1-5df1-861b-513da03dbaea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345598} {:answer/id #uuid "3defc48d-d7e1-5b0e-b671-1730d06d90b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345599} {:answer/id #uuid "a61af580-9f8a-5aaf-ad73-ff407a9c1323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345600} {:answer/id #uuid "1873399a-0706-582f-933c-ee6a5de25d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345601} {:answer/id #uuid "888f0ec7-4c54-5724-9db3-d0bed4c700bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1873399a-0706-582f-933c-ee6a5de25d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345601} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c3f3166-876e-5faa-b246-3af0a54b9237" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0a25f22-3baf-52b8-9a99-46b0db20c803" :question/math-academy-id "q-90699" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+16y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+16(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+16(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)}^{′}-{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)-(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡4x+{sin}^{2}⁡4x) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not equal to $0,$ the functions are linearly independent, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$  is the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67ca0bc-c946-5963-a733-2ec9eb6b07ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb9f878c-8b1b-54d6-a4a5-2ba40060eb6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345605} {:answer/id #uuid "9c1ef5dc-b268-5a88-958f-63b1dfd1ed37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345606} {:answer/id #uuid "6b70e8b5-951e-5d5e-b12e-7ea7f5d32d9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345607} {:answer/id #uuid "2995f742-2afa-5c2f-ba31-0f9ef6ab4534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345608} {:answer/id #uuid "29817ece-f842-5144-b0bd-59a6dafe4c6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Bx\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2995f742-2afa-5c2f-ba31-0f9ef6ab4534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345608} :answer-field/id #uuid "d84b5e57-e0e0-54d9-8718-3c467c0324be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6142e9d9-60af-53a0-80f6-f2464029d408" :question/math-academy-id "q-90911" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=6w\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ becomes \n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=3\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$"}]}
